.TH "POKEMON-COLORSCRIPTS" "1" "2026-10-16" "GNU" "User Commands"

.SH "NAME"
.PP
pokemon-colorscripts - display unicode sprites of pokemon in the terminal

.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.PP
.B pokemon-colorscripts
.RB [ -h | --help ]
.RB [ -l | --list ]
.RB [ -n | --name
.IR name ]
.RB [ -d | --dex
.IR DEX ]
.RB [ -r | --random
.RI [ GEN ]]
.RB [ --daily ]
.RB [ -f | --form
.RI [ FORM ]]
.RB [ -s | --shiny ]
.RB [ -c | --compact ]
.RB [ --no-title ]

.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
Pokemon-colorscripts is a CLI utility written in Python that prints out
unicode sprites of pokemon to the terminal. Pokemon from generations 1-10 and
alternate forms are available. You can select specific pokemon by name or
Pokedex number, choose a random pokemon, or show a daily selection unique to
the date and machine. Name, dex, random, and daily are mutually exclusive
targets. With no target the command prints help, except that \fB-f\fR
implicitly selects a random pokemon. Random selection can be limited to one
generation or a range/list of generations.

.SH "OPTIONS"
.TP
.B "-h, --help"
Print help.
.TP
.B "-l, --list"
List all available pokemon. Combine with \fB-f\fR to list pokemon and their
forms, with \fB-l -n name\fR or \fB-l -d dex\fR to list one pokemon's forms,
or with \fB-l -r [GEN]\fR to list a randomly selected pokemon with alternate
forms. These target options work with or without \fB-f\fR when its value is
omitted. \fB-l -f\fR without a value lists every pokemon and all its forms,
including the regular form. \fB-l -f form\fR lists pokemon with any form
matching the case-insensitive regular expression \fIform\fR, then displays all
their forms; the match may include the regular form. A valued \fB-f\fR cannot
be combined with \fB-l -n\fR, \fB-l -d\fR, or \fB-l -r\fR. The display
modifiers \fB--shiny\fR, \fB--compact\fR, and \fB--no-title\fR do not change
list output.
.TP
\fB -n, --name\fR \fI NAME\fR
Select a pokemon by a name shown by \fB--list\fR. Names must match the listed
spelling. A few names use special spellings such as nidoran-f, nidoran-m,
farfetchd, and mr-mime.
.TP
\fB -d, --dex\fR \fI DEX\fR
Select a pokemon by Pokedex number, from 1 through the number of pokemon in
the installed data. Form selection and display modifiers work the same as with
\fB--name\fR.
.TP
\fB -r, --random\fR \fI[GEN]\fR
Select a pokemon at random and print it out. GEN may be a generation number
from 1-10, an inclusive range such as 1-3, or a comma-separated list such as
1,3,6. If omitted, all generations are eligible. For a comma-separated list,
one listed generation is chosen at random, then a pokemon is selected from
that generation.
With \fB-f\fR and no value, it selects a random form, including regular, from
a pokemon that has alternate forms. With
\fB-f FORM\fR, it selects a random pokemon that has a form matching the
case-insensitive regular expression \fIform\fR, then selects a matching form.
When a pokemon is selected randomly with \fB-r\fR or implicit \fB--form\fR,
it has a 1 in 128 chance of being shiny unless \fB--shiny\fR is specified.
.TP
\fB --daily\fR
Show the same pokemon, form, and shiny state throughout the current day on the
same machine. The selection is based on the date and hostname and has a
deterministic 1-in-128 chance of being shiny. Only \fB--compact\fR and
\fB--no-title\fR may be used as modifiers; it cannot be combined with
\fB--list\fR, \fB--form\fR, \fB--shiny\fR, or another target.
.TP
\fB -f, --form\fR \fI[FORM]\fR
Select a form using the case-insensitive regular expression \fIFORM\fR, which
is searched against form names. With \fB--name\fR or \fB--dex\fR and a value,
a matching form name is selected directly; otherwise, a matching form is
selected at random. If there is no match, the pokemon's forms are listed. With
\fB--random\fR and a value, a pokemon with a matching form and then one of its
matching forms are selected at random. Without a value, \fB-f\fR with
\fB--name\fR or \fB--dex\fR selects from all forms, including regular; with
\fB--random\fR or implicit random selection, it selects from all forms,
including regular, of pokemon that have alternate forms. Without \fB-f\fR,
\fB--random\fR selects the regular form. With a value and no target, it
implicitly selects a random pokemon from generations 1-10. In list mode, see
the \fB--list\fR description.
.TP
\fB -s, --shiny\fR
Show the shiny version of the pokemon. Random selections also have a 1 in 128
chance of being shiny when this option is not specified. \fB--daily\fR uses
its own deterministic chance and does not accept this option.
.TP
\fB -c, --compact\fR
Show a smaller sprite. Larger sprites are shown by default.
.TP
\fB --no-title\fR
Do not display pokemon name

.SH "FILES"
.TP
All the files are present in the directory
"/usr/local/opt/pokemon-colorscripts"
.TP
with the executable script being symlinked to
"/usr/local/bin/pokemon-colorscripts"

.SH "EXAMPLES"
.SS "pokemon-colorscripts -r"
.PP
Prints out a random pokemon. Add this command to your shell config file (.bashrc, .zshrc, fish.config
etc.) to print out a random pokemon every time you open the terminal.
.SS "pokemon-colorscripts -r 1"
.PP
Print out a random pokemon from generation 1.
.SS "pokemon-colorscripts -r 1-3"
.PP
Print out a random pokemon from generations 1-3.
.SS "pokemon-colorscripts -r 1,3,6"
.PP
Print out a random pokemon from generations 1,3 or 6.
.SS "pokemon-colorscripts -l"
.PP
Print out all available pokemon names.
.SS "pokemon-colorscripts -n chikorita"
.PP
Print out the pokemon chikorita.
.SS "pokemon-colorscripts -n mudkip -s"
.PP
Print out the shiny version of mudkip.
.SS "pokemon-colorscripts -n charizard -f"
.PP
Print out a random form of charizard.
.SS "pokemon-colorscripts --form mega"
.PP
Print out a random pokemon and a random form whose name matches "mega".
.SS "pokemon-colorscripts -r -f mega"
.PP
Print out a random pokemon with a form whose name matches "mega".
.SS "pokemon-colorscripts -l -f"
.PP
List all pokemon and all their forms, including the regular form.
.SS "pokemon-colorscripts -l -f mega"
.PP
List pokemon with any form whose name matches "mega", displaying all of their
forms.
